migration.text:
Feature: use the OIDC picture claim as the user's avatar.

When the identity provider sends a picture claim, OpenIDProvider.authenticate() should download that image on login and make it the user's profile image: converted to webp and stored as PrivateUser.get_directory(user_id) / "profile.webp", the way an uploaded profile image is, with the user's cache_key rotated so clients reload it.

Re-download only when the claim changes: store sha256(url).hexdigest() of the claim the avatar was built from in a new nullable string column users.external_avatar_hash (Alembic migration, the User model, the PrivateUser schema, the frontend's PrivateUser type), and skip the download when the stored value matches. A claim that is absent, empty or not a string is ignored.

The URL is untrusted input (many providers let users edit their own profile), so:
- on the provider's own host (the host of OIDC_CONFIGURATION_URL) the image is fetched as it is, over HTTP or HTTPS, on any address (self-hosted providers live on the LAN);
- on any other host the URL must be https and its host must resolve (socket.getaddrinfo) only to public addresses: no loopback, private, link-local or unique-local address, and a host that does not resolve is refused. The check runs before any request is made;
- redirects are walked one hop at a time (requests.get(..., allow_redirects=False)), each hop checked by the same rules before it is requested; a relative Location is resolved against the current URL, a redirect without a Location is refused, and at most MAX_PICTURE_REDIRECTS redirects are followed (a module-level constant: at most MAX_PICTURE_REDIRECTS + 1 requests in all);
- the body is capped at MAX_PICTURE_BYTES (a constant of the module that defines OpenIDProvider, like MAX_PICTURE_REDIRECTS; it uses that module's socket, requests and shutil imports, which tests replace): a larger declared Content-Length is refused, and so is a stream that grows past it whatever the header said;
- the download goes to a temporary file that is moved into place with shutil.move, so nothing is left in the temp directory.

An image that fails any check, a failed download, an image that cannot be decoded, or a claim that breaks URL parsing or name resolution is skipped and logged: the login still succeeds, and cache_key and external_avatar_hash stay as they were. Document the behaviour on the OIDC documentation page.

Tests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 9 minutes).
